WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.850
I am here to talk about time,
which is obviously everyone's

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.800
favorite subject. In particular,

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.290
I'm actually here to talk
about a package called clock.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.190
So clock is a date time
manipulation library

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.500
Kind of in the same way that
lubridate is a date time

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.980
manipulation library.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.490
It does things you might
expect add dates, subtract

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.240
dates, format and parse them.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.330
All kinds of other manipulation.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.850
If you get anything
out of this talk,

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.700
it's really that
clock is not here

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.930
to replace lubridate in any way.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.740
The only idea would
be that in the end

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.980
clock might be a back end
for lubridate in the same way

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.830
that dtplyr or dbplyr
are different types

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.118
of back ends for dplyr.

00:00:46.118 --> 00:00:48.660
And I'm not even going to spend
the rest of this talk talking

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.060
about features that
overlap with lubridate.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.830
Instead, I want to
talk about things that

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.000
are pretty unique to clock.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.700
One of those is safety.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.540
And one of those is calendars.
Because I only have 5 minutes,

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:07.970
I'm going to do that with one
date, January 30th of this year.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.970
Safety is built into
clock from the ground up

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.180
to hopefully avoid issues
like this, time zone

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.000
issues, invalid date  issues,
things that are pretty common

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.500
when you're working
with time series

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.150
and just drive you up the wall.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.310
So let's jump into safety.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.630
Here's a timeline.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.600
This is January 30th,
our date in question

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.590
marked in blue on our timeline.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.450
It continues
through to February.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.290
On the next line, you'll see
this gap between February

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.380
and March because
February only has 28 days,

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.670
but January had 31, so it
doesn't necessarily map 1 to 1.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:46.020
If I were to ask you this
seemingly innocuous question.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.050
Please add one
month to this date.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.650
What would you get?

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.960
Well, if we were
to ask lubridate,

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.820
it gives you a somewhat
reasonable answer of NA.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.410
There is nothing that maps
1 to 1 from January 30th

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:03.050
to something in February, maybe.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.450
And there's nothing
particularly wrong with this

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:10.100
except for the fact that it's
not the most useful answer.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.200
Generally, you'll
be running this code

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.830
and it happens silently.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.690
And then five steps downstream.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.500
All of a sudden, you discover
there's some NAs here.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.300
Like, I didn't have
those to begin with.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.440
Where did those come from?

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.690
And you have to backtrack
up through your calculations

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.020
and figure out why they appeared.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.890
If you were to ask clock
this question with add months,

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.380
it actually gives you an
error in this special case

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.580
by default. It
says, whoa, hold up.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:36.930
There's something wrong here.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.600
Go look at location 1.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.320
If you had a vector, it might be
location five, seven, whatever.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.210
And check out the
invalid argument

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.340
to learn more about this case.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.680
You go and you look
at the documentation

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.690
and you come out with
the idea that maybe I

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.050
could set this thing called
invalid equals previous.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.580
That allows you to say, give
me the previous valid date when

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:00.270
I have this kind of problem.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.740
That's the end of February.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.910
I think that's a pretty
reasonable result in this case.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:06.830
But you also might
want to say, depending

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:10.640
on your specific problem,
invalid equals next to map

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.750
forward to the
beginning of March instead.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:16.220
if you actually do like
that lubridate behavior,

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.070
that's fine.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.820
You can say invalid equals
NA any time that occurs,

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.680
you'll get an NA instead.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.600
So that's about safety.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.970
Let's talk about calendars.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.580
Calendars are just
the idea of a way

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.550
to represent a
unique point in time.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.350
With our date in
question, we could

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.950
use a calendar called
year month day

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.090
to represent this date using
three components the year,

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.110
the month, and the
day of the month.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.360
But this isn't the only way
you could represent this date.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:50.470
You could also use the year
and the day of the year,

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:54.580
or you could use one of these
many other calendar types that

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.380
are built into clock.

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:57.880
If your finance
person, you might

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.010
be particularly interested
in year quarter day,

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:04.230
which uses a true fiscal
year to represent your date.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:08.710
These are really nice
because they're all

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:10.370
convertible to each other.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:12.790
You can work with any
particular calendar type

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:14.473
and say you need to
get the quarter out.

00:04:14.473 --> 00:04:17.140
You convert to year quarter day,
you do manipulation over there,

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.019
you convert back.

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:21.959
It's obviously convertible
with the date in POSIXct

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:24.540
as well, since those are the
date time types that you're

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.980
most likely to start out with.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.690
The other really
neat thing that I

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:32.420
find really fun about
these calendar types

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:35.780
is that they have what's
known as variable precision.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:39.440
These are all day precision
calendar types at this point,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.260
but we could narrow that down
to month precision as needed.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:47.320
And you've got a built-in
year month type in clock.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.350
Similarly, you could have a
built-in year quarter type.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.190
You can actually go
the other way, too.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:58.400
You can widen it out all the way
to nanoseconds if you need it.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.530
The last thing I'll
say is that clock

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.110
is completely compatible with
some of the other packages

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:04.610
you might be familiar
with that I've

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:07.850
created called slider and IVs.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:10.460
Slider is one for
rolling averages,

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:13.640
so you can use clock
types as the index to say,

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.030
give me a rolling average.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.810
looking back four
or five quarters

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:20.212
IVs is a relatively new package.

00:05:20.212 --> 00:05:21.920
You might not have
heard of this one yet,

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.650
but it deals with
date ranges and you

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:29.470
can use clock types as the
components of those ranges.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.460
So to sum up, lubridate
is not going anywhere.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.740
Don't worry, but please try
clock for enhanced safety

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.460
in these powerful new types.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.330
Thank you.

